{"kind":"report","text":"Independent Architecture/Security/Simplification review of exact head 3fd75543f80c42b400fdd446e1c5b4524e39e200 on request/decision-records-ui-current, verified from a clean checkout of that head (#11617).\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers: the layer 7 surface (ui/ and its rebuilt uidist) and the layer 5/7 derivation boundary; kernel and application contracts are unchanged, and internal/service carries the rebuilt bundle only. The boundary all four dissent defects lived on is now written down in this same head — \"Layer 5 and layer 7: what the browser may derive\" in docs/reference/architecture.md: layer 7 may read and combine projected fields for presentation, but may not name a relation layer 5 does not project. Adoption stays unprojected and ungated; the surface row of the dependency table is updated to match. Contract preserved, with the candidate artifact published where the contract lives.\n\nSECURITY. The authority gate fails closed: mayRatify refuses when the viewer, the live vocabulary, or the kind definition is absent, and recognises exactly the satisfier forms the fold itself judges by — role:\u003cname\u003e checked against the projected roster, originating-requester checked against the request author while still rostered. Enforcement stays server-side in the fold; the gate withdraws a misleading affordance and grants nothing. No new injection surface (no dangerouslySetInnerHTML, innerHTML or eval); credentials stay memory-only; the modal listener is capture-phase on the owner document and removed on cleanup. The committed uidist hashes match a fresh make ui-check build. All four closures were verified by reverting each fix alone and watching its own test fail: forcing mayRatify true fails \"a ratification is offered only to an actor the fold would let ratify\"; removing the bound fails \"the citation prefill is bounded however many proposals qualify\"; removing the sequence ordering fails \"the cited proposal is chosen by the fold's sequence, not by serialisation order\"; neutering the modal keydown handler fails the publish-dialog focus-contract test at \"Tab past the last control wraps to the first\", exercised through a real gs serve. The two changed pre-existing tests had encoded the authority defect (empty actor map, no vocabulary, ratify offered); both now supply a ratifier and the vocabulary, which strengthens them. Gates at the exact tree: gofmt clean; go build ./... and go vet ./... ok; go test -count=1 ./... all 31 packages ok; make ui-check green with internal/service/uidist porcelain empty afterwards; npm test 94/94; git diff --check clean.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The cap of one collapses cap and ordering into a single decision — sound, documented, and reversible by a one-constant edit if a future world wants more. The record index replaces hundreds of full-projection scans per screen with one build — justified, not fluff. Nothing to cut.\n\nFOR THE RECORD. JoinGate still declares aria-modal without useModalFocus; correctly left outside planner's four conditions and wanting its own child request. npm test now requires the Go toolchain on PATH because publish-chain.test.mjs builds cmd/gs — disclosed by the implementer, confirmed locally here, not verified on any CI image. The branch-name deviation (request/decision-records-ui-current where planner named request/decision-records-ui) is the implementer's flagged judgment for planner to rule on; the reviewed head itself is exact, clean and pushed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:862e8bb52c7dc69ecb07898f7eae2ff6f8efa82b","head":"3fd75543f80c42b400fdd446e1c5b4524e39e200","verdict":"approved"}}